        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Populism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1880 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val">Hélio Jaguaribe, whose 1954 text is considered the first academic study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> populism; Edward Shils, who proposed populism in 1954 as a term for anti-elite trends in US society; Richard Hofstadter and Daniel Bell, who read the People's Party as status anxiety; Gino Germani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seymour-martin-lipset">Seymour Martin Lipset</a>, on mass mobilisation; Ernesto Laclau of the Essex School, who treats populism as a discursive logic; Cas Mudde and Cristóbal Rovira Kaltwasser, who call it a thin-centred ideology; Kurt Weyland, who defines it as a political strategy</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Léon Lemonnier's 1929 manifesto of the populist novel; the National People's Party's first national platform of 1892; Hélio Jaguaribe's 1954 study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> populism; Edward Shils's 1954 article on anti-elite trends in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">a society divided between the pure people and a corrupt elite; the general will of the people; the common sense of the people set above expert and institutional knowledge; distrust of representative institutions and a preference for direct decisions such as referendums; a charismatic leader as the voice of the people; a style of politics that breaks with the manners of the established elite</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">The term is applied to parties and leaders on both left and right, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriza">Syriza</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s Podemos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Aam Aadmi Party, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s Freedom Party, the Swiss People's Party and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicialist-party">Justicialist Party</a>, and scholars still disagree over whether it is a useful category at all</span></div>
