    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Populism has no single agreed definition: scholars treat it as a thin-centred ideology, a class phenomenon, a discursive logic, a political style, a strategy for winning power or a pattern of economic policy, and some have proposed abandoning the word.</li><li>The word first appeared in English in 1858 and took hold through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> People's Party of the 1880s to early 1900s, while the Russian narodniki of the 1860s and 1870s, often translated as populists, differed from it in ideology and course.</li><li>Scholars sharply disagree on its relation to democracy: some define it as inherently anti-democratic, others as a democratic impulse that empowers marginalised groups, and others hold that it takes contradictory forms depending on context.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Between 1950 and 1960 roughly 160 publications on populism were recorded, and by 2018 the number of academic papers and books with the term in their title or abstract had grown to 615 in a single year; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> Dictionary chose &quot;populism&quot; as its Word of the Year in 2017.</p></aside>
    </section>

