    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">The word &quot;populist&quot; first appears in English, as an antonym for &quot;aristocratic&quot; in a translation of a work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1892</span><span class="rf-text">The National People's Party adopts its first national platform in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which calls for protecting the rights of urban workmen</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">The People's Party supports the Democratic candidate William Jennings Bryan, and its support plunges after his defeat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1929</span><span class="rf-text">Léon Lemonnier publishes a manifesto for the populist novel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1945</span><span class="rf-text">The mass mobilisation of 17 October in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, often considered the founding moment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1954</span><span class="rf-text">Edward Shils in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Hélio Jaguaribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> publish the texts that turn populism into an academic concept</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1967</span><span class="rf-text">A conference at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics fails to produce a unified theory of populism</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2016</span><span class="rf-text">The election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-trump">Donald Trump</a> and the United Kingdom's vote to leave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> are widely read as expressions of populist sentiment</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine.jpg" alt="Alphonse de Lamartine" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alphonse_de_Lamartine_par_Fran%C3%A7ois_G%C3%A9rard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Gérard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The word "populist" first appeared in English in 1858, as an antonym for "aristocratic" in a translation of a work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> of the 1860s and 1870s the term was associated with the narodniki, a left-leaning agrarian movement whose name is often translated as "populists". It aimed to transfer political power to the peasant communes through a radical programme of agrarian reform, and some historians have described it as an intellectual and organisational precursor of later revolutionary movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In English the term gained broader prominence through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> People's Party and its predecessors, active between the 1880s and the early 1900s. The party championed small-scale farmers, advocated expansionist monetary policies and accessible credit, and was relatively progressive for its time on questions of women's and minority rights.</p><p>Although both the Russian and the American movements have been labelled populist, they differed in ideological content and historical trajectory. In early 20th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the term moved into literature, naming a genre of novel that portrayed the lives of the lower classes sympathetically: Léon Lemonnier published a manifesto for it in 1929 and Antonine Coullet-Tessier established a prize for it in 1931. Some scholars have also retrospectively associated populism with the democracy of classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levellers">Levellers</a> of seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Because the term is used for a broad and often contradictory array of movements, there is no single doctrine to describe, and the main theoretical approaches define it differently. The ideational approach of Cas Mudde and Cristóbal Rovira Kaltwasser defines populism as a "thin-centred ideology" that divides society into two antagonistic groups, "the pure people" and "the corrupt elite", and sees politics as an expression of the general will of the people. It attaches itself to broader movements such as socialism or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>, and so appears on both the left and the right. In this view the people's common sense is held to be superior to elite expertise, and representative democracy is criticised in favour of direct decisions such as referendums.</p><p>Other approaches put the weight elsewhere. Ernesto Laclau treated populism as a discursive logic in which unmet demands coalesce around a symbol that names a popular movement against an elite. The performative approach of Pierre Ostiguy, Benjamin Moffitt and María Esperanza Casullo sees a political style: rhetoric, gestures and staging that break with the norms of respectable politics. Kurt Weyland defined it as a strategy of a personalist leader who governs through direct, unmediated and uninstitutionalised support from large numbers of mostly unorganised followers. Economists such as Rudiger Dornbusch, Jeffrey Sachs and Sebastián Edwards used the word for expansive public spending financed by foreign loans and followed by inflationary crises and austerity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas.jpg" alt="Getúlio Vargas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Getulio_Vargas_(1930).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Governo do Brasil, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> populism was often characterised by regional mobilisation and loose organisation. In the late 19th and early 20th centuries it was widespread in the western provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and in the southwest and Great Plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where it combined with agrarianism as "prairie populism" and set small independent farmers against the bankers and politicians of the northeast. Later American examples included the Greenback Party, Robert M. La Follette's Progressive Party of 1924 and Huey P. Long's Share Our Wealth movement of 1933 to 1935, and in Canada the social credit groups of the 1930s to the 1960s. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> right-wing populist parties emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, among them Guglielmo Giannini's Common Man's Front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Pierre Poujade's union in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> the term entered political language after the war. A first wave, lasting until the 1960s, brought leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas">Getúlio Vargas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Domingo Perón</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>; a second, in the late 1980s and early 1990s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-collor-de-mello">Fernando Collor de Mello</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alberto-fujimori">Alberto Fujimori</a>; a third, from the late 1990s, the left-wing governments of the pink tide. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, populism became apparent in the 1980s according to Danielle Resnick, with military leaders such as Jerry Rawlings in Ghana and Thomas Sankara in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>. In South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> populist politicians emerged after the 1997 Asian financial crisis, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the term has been applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> mobilisation and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharatiya-janata-party">Bharatiya Janata Party</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill.jpg" alt="John Stuart Mill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Stuart_Mill_by_London_Stereoscopic_Company,_c1870.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">London Stereoscopic Company, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The record of populists in power differs by case and by definition. Ideational scholars note that not all populists are authoritarian and that populism in opposition can mobilise groups who feel excluded and draw attention to popular grievances, but they generally contend that it becomes detrimental to pluralism once in power. By claiming to represent the authentic will of the people, they argue, populists, particularly on the right, may concentrate executive power and bypass or undermine institutions such as the judiciary and the media, a dynamic that can give rise to what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> called the "tyranny of the majority".</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, Vargas and Perón organised labour unions and drew previously excluded groups into national politics. Marxist critics such as Francisco Weffort, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-henrique-cardoso">Fernando Henrique Cardoso</a> and Octavio Ianni acknowledged this integration but argued that it stayed within a bourgeois framework and that populist regimes subordinated trade unions to state control; other historians argue that the period saw growing politicisation of workers. Menem, Collor and Fujimori combined anti-elite rhetoric with neoliberal reforms, often in line with recommendations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Hugo Chávez</a> promoted participatory structures such as Communal Councils. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, Rawlings and Sankara came to power in coups, claiming to represent the voice of the people and setting up participatory organisations. In South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> the populist administrations of Joseph Estrada, Roh Moo-hyun, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-shui-bian">Chen Shui-bian</a> and Thaksin Shinawatra were removed soon after taking power. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the People's Party won seats in several state legislatures in the 1890s but never mounted a successful presidential challenge.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Criticism of populism and criticism of the concept run together. In the 1950s and 1960s Richard Hofstadter and Daniel Bell reinterpreted the People's Party through a critical lens as an expression of status anxiety and irrationalism, and compared the anti-elitism of the 1890s Populists with that of Joseph McCarthy; not all academics accepted the comparison. Liberal scholars such as Steven Levitsky and Daniel Ziblatt argue that authoritarian-leaning populists become viable only when traditional elites accommodate them, and that backsliding follows when elites abandon informal norms of mutual toleration. Karl Loewenstein's idea of militant democracy holds that democracies may sometimes take exceptional restrictive measures to defend themselves. Mudde and Rovira Kaltwasser, though critical of populism, caution against dismissing populists as irrational, since this reinforces the binary of pure people and corrupt elite.</p><p>Each definition has its own critics. The ideational approach is said to proceed deductively and to impose assumptions of moral dualism; the strategic approach, by focusing on leaders, excludes parties and movements such as the People's Party; the economic definition, critics argue, reduces populism to left-wing mismanagement and stigmatises heterodox policies. Scholars influenced by Laclau hold that populism is inherently emancipatory and pluralistic, and that authoritarian nationalist movements often called populist are better described as fascist. Some scholars have proposed abandoning the word, since its conflation with far-right nativism misrepresents self-described historical populists, while others hold the concept too integral to political analysis to discard.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Until the 1950s academics used the word mostly for historians of the People's Party. Edward Shils's article of 1954 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mccarthyism">McCarthyism</a> and Hélio Jaguaribe's text of the same year on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> opened its wider use, and a conference at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> School of Economics in 1967 failed to produce a shared theory. About 160 publications on populism were recorded between 1950 and 1960 and over 1,500 between 1990 and 2000. After the political events of 2016 the term gained unprecedented global prominence, and the annual count of papers with it in the title or abstract rose from 95 on average in 2000 to 2015 to 615 in 2018. Its use has taken a more negative tone, and it has often been conflated with demagoguery.</p><p>Scholars have proposed many causes, among them economic grievance, the losses of modernisation, a cultural backlash against postmaterialist values, post-democracy and the media, with mixed empirical support. The protests that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a>, including Occupy, the Indignados and the Gilets jaunes, have been described as populist, as have parties that grew out of them, such as Podemos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Aam Aadmi Party. Some European states have adopted a cordon sanitaire, in which mainstream parties refuse to form coalitions with populist or extremist actors. On the left, Chantal Mouffe argues for a left populist project that mobilises passion for democratic and egalitarian ends.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Populism is a contested concept for politics that set the common people against a perceived elite. The word dates from 1858 and spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> People's Party of the 1880s to early 1900s; scholars define it as an ideology, a style, a strategy or an economic pattern, and disagree on how it relates to democracy.</em></p>

